# PR#379: half fixed, half refused. The half that was a real defect is fixed: a cursor
# write that failed used to send the entry back through the retry ladder and re-run a
# handler that had already committed, which turned a Redis hiccup into a certain
# duplicate rather than a possible one. Making the cursor atomic with the handler is the
# refused half. It cannot be done: the acknowledgement is a Redis call either way, so a
# database transaction cannot contain it, and the handler this finding names as the
# victim, Raw, writes nothing to the database at all, so there is no transaction for a
# cursor to join. Delivery over a stream is at least once, and this layer answers that
# where the answer belongs, in the handlers: a message is deduped by its source id, a
# status only moves forwards, and group and connection state is last write wins. What is
# left is a repeated PROVIDER_EVENT_RECEIVED webhook after a crash in the window between
# a commit and a SET, and a processed-events table to prevent it would cost a write per
# event on the ordering-critical path.
app/services/whatsapp/connector/consumer/shard_worker.rb: Commit event progress atomically with handler effects  # PR#379
# PR#380: real, known and not what this slice can fix. The webhook transport carries no
# ordering, which is what fazer-ai/chatwoot#373 records and what the job this finding
# names documents at its top: the payloads have nothing monotonic to rebuild an order
# from, so the only thing on offer is waiting, and waiting is what `:deferred` does for
# the case a target that is not stored yet. The remedy asked for here, serializing every
# event of a session or a chat, is a different inbound design (one queue per session, or
# a per-chat cursor with something to compare), not a change to this job. The reaction
# sub-case has a cheaper answer that is worse: deferring a removal that finds no active
# reaction would spend the whole retry ladder on every stray removal, which is the
# ordinary shape (a reaction taken back on a message from before the inbox was connected)
# in exchange for a race between two webhooks that are usually seconds apart.
